From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brandon Williams <turbine.bmw@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 18:45:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] soundwire: qcom: re-enumerate the Surface Pro 11's controller
 on runtime resume

The Surface Pro 11's speaker bus, a v2.0.0 controller with two WSA884x
amplifiers, comes back from a clock stop the way 2438 describes on
Glymur: now and then a bus clash at the wake, after which one amplifier
answers every register read with 0x21. The PA health check then reads
a fault and leaves that amplifier disabled, and on this machine it did
not recover: on every later stream for the rest of the hour the right
amplifier reported a real PA fault (PA_FSM_STA1 0x4, ERR_COND0 0x20) and
was switched off about 0.6 s in, so the right speaker went quiet.

Take 2438's path on the Surface Pro 11 too: no clock stop, and a soft
reset and enumeration on runtime resume. Playing a quiet burst after
every runtime suspend and reading the amplifiers right at the wake, for
an hour each:

  stock:      158 wakes; 0x21 reads left the right amplifier disabled
              on 2, and from the second on it was switched off for a
              PA fault on about 150
  this patch: 156 wakes, no PA fault

Bus clashes and parity errors at the wake still occur with it, but no
longer leave an amplifier stuck.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
---
 drivers/soundwire/qcom.c | 10 +++++++---
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/soundwire/qcom.c b/drivers/soundwire/qcom.c
--- a/drivers/soundwire/qcom.c
+++ b/drivers/soundwire/qcom.c
@@ -667,10 +667,14 @@
 		/*
 		 * Peripherals on v3.1.0 controllers (Glymur) often come back
 		 * from a clock stop unattached, or attached but returning
-		 * garbage for every register read. Reset the controller and
-		 * re-enumerate on runtime resume instead, as at probe.
+		 * garbage for every register read. So do the Surface Pro 11's
+		 * WSA884x amplifiers on its v2.0.0 controller. Reset the
+		 * controller and re-enumerate on runtime resume instead, as at
+		 * probe.
 		 */
-		ctrl->clock_stop_not_supported = ctrl->version >= SWRM_VERSION_3_1_0;
+		ctrl->clock_stop_not_supported =
+			ctrl->version >= SWRM_VERSION_3_1_0 ||
+			of_machine_is_compatible("microsoft,denali");
 		/* Now compare with entries */
 		list_for_each_entry_safe(slave, _s, &bus->slaves, node) {
 			if (sdw_compare_devid(slave, id) == 0) {
